<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hispania Ulterior</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/197-bce">197 BCE</a>–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Further Spain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Ulterior ('Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>') was a Roman province created in 197 BCE in the south and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Baetis</a> (Guadalquivir) valley, with Corduba as its main city. Under Augustus it was divided into the provinces of Baetica and Lusitania. Its rich mines and farmland made it one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s most valuable western territories.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> expanded into the south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> after losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>. Much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> between Carthage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> took control from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and the Carthaginians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilipa">Battle of Ilipa</a> in 206 BCE. Carthage ceded the region to Rome after its defeat in 201 BCE. In 197 BCE the peninsula was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>, Nearer Hispania, and Hispania Ulterior, Further Hispania, because two Roman military forces were present during the conquest. The boundary ran roughly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> to the Cantabrian Sea.</p><p>Peace lasted until 155 BCE, when the Lusitanians attacked, twice defeating Roman praetors and encouraging other rebellions. The peninsula became a centre of military activity and a chance for advancement, as the historian Appian noted of consuls who sought glory, gain or a triumph. The consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimus-junius-brutus-callaicus">Decimus Junius Brutus Callaicus</a> largely conquered the area in 138 BCE, but fighting went on until 19 BCE, when Agrippa defeated the Cantabrians. Gaius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> governed Hispania Ulterior in 61 and 60 BCE. In 19 BCE Augustus reorganised the provinces, dividing Hispania Ulterior into Baetica and Lusitania, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> was renamed Hispania Tarraconensis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Each province was ruled by a praetor. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, the peoples of the region came from many tribes and shared neither a language nor a government. Members of the tribal elites were brought into the Roman aristocracy and allowed to take part in their own governance, and Roman latifundia were granted to aristocrats. The emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, Hadrian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> were all born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. The economy thrived as a granary and through exports of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wool and wine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> is attested from at least 200 BCE, when the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Quintus Ennius</a> used it, and may derive from a Punic phrase meaning coast of hyraxes, a Phoenician misidentification of the peninsula's many rabbits. Ulterior is the comparative of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word meaning beyond. In the early fifth century CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> invaded and took the south of Hispania. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> commissioned the Visigoth king Athaulf to defeat them, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> took control and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> their capital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hispania Ulterior” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their lands formed part of the Republican province of <b>Hispania Ulterior</b>, and campaigns by Decimus Junius Brutus in 138–137 BCE, by Julius Caesar as governor in 61–60 BCE and by others completed the conquest, interrupted by the war of Sertorius in the 70s BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of Carthage the Iberian territories were divided into two provinces, <b>Hispania Ulterior</b> and Hispania Citerior.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory became the Roman provinces of Hispania Citerior and <b>Ulterior</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a></li><li><blockquote>Combining Roman exiles with Lusitanian and Celtiberian warriors, he defeated Sulla's generals and by 77 BCE controlled much of Hispania Citerior and <b>Ulterior</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sertorian-state">Sertorian State</a></li></ul></section>
